**Definition 1.1 (Affine amplitude model and sources).** The reconstruction and consequences below adapt Haobo Ma and Wenlin Zhang, *Proof of Critch–Morton Conjecture 8: reconstruction from any consecutive three-site improper marginal*, v2, 3 November 2026, © 2026 the creators, [CC BY 4.0](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/). Ma's supplied affiliation is ChronoAI Pte Ltd, Singapore; Zhang's are National University of Singapore and ChronoAI Pte Ltd, Singapore. The posing source is Andrew Critch and Jason Morton, *Algebraic geometry of matrix product states*, SIGMA **10** (2014), 095, [doi:10.3842/SIGMA.2014.095](https://doi.org/10.3842/SIGMA.2014.095), §2, equation (2), Theorem 7 and Conjecture 8. The conjecture already occurs in §II, p. 4 of [arXiv:1210.2812v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/1210.2812v1) (10 October 2012), titled *Polynomial constraints on representing entangled qubits as matrix product states*. The [v4 text](https://arxiv.org/abs/1210.2812v4) agrees with the journal's model and conjecture.

For $A_0,A_1\in\mathbb C^{2\times2}$, a row $\alpha\in\mathbb C^{1\times2}$,
and a column $\beta\in\mathbb C^{2\times1}$, write
$$
\tag{1}
 \psi_w=\alpha A_{w_1}\cdots A_{w_N}\beta,
 \qquad w\in\{0,1\}^N.
$$
Let $X_N=\overline{\operatorname{OB}(2,2,N)}$ be the affine Zariski closure of these
amplitude tensors. This is the model of Critch and Morton [Critch–Morton, Section 2,
equation (2)]. For $0\leq a\leq N-3$, put $b=N-3-a$ and define
$$
\tag{2}
 (\mu_a\psi)_{ijk}=\sum_{u\in\{0,1\}^a}\sum_{v\in\{0,1\}^b}\psi_{uijkv}.
$$
All coordinates are affine complex amplitudes. The same pair of matrices occurs at every site, with independent boundary vectors. There is no norm constraint or phase quotient, and open boundaries do not impose cyclic invariance. The contraction is complex bilinear, with no complex conjugation; it is an amplitude sum rather than a partial trace of a density operator.
With $S=A_0+A_1$, distributivity gives the useful identity
$$
\tag{3}
 (\mu_a\psi)_{ijk}=\alpha S^a A_iA_jA_k S^b\beta.
$$
In particular $\mu_a:X_N\to X_3$ is a polynomial map: it maps the parametrized
model to the three-site model, and polynomial equations extend to its closure.

**Theorem 1.** 
For every $N\geq4$ and $0\leq a\leq N-3$, the map $\mu_a:X_N\to X_3$
is birational. More precisely, there is a polynomial $g$ in the eight
three-site coordinates such that $U=X_3\cap\{g\ne0\}$ is nonempty, and a
rational map $F_{N,a}:U\to X_N$ is inverse to $\mu_a$ on
$X_N\cap\{g\circ\mu_a\ne0\}$.
The vanishing ideals of $X_N$ and $X_3$ are prime.

